{"name":"Mondrian Blue","hex":"#0f478c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Piet Mondrian","blocks":[{"text":"Pieter Cornelis Mondriaan, known after 1911 as Piet Mondrian, was a Dutch painter and art theoretician, who is regarded as one of the greatest artists of the 20th century. He was one of the pioneers of 20th-century abstract art, as he changed his artistic direction from figurative painting to an increasingly abstract style, until he reached a point where his artistic vocabulary was taken down to simple geometric elements."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Piet_Mondriaan.jpg/330px-Piet_Mondriaan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piet_Mondrian","wikidata":"Q151803"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Mondriaan_huis_Amersfoort_2.JPG/500px-Mondriaan_huis_Amersfoort_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Mondriaan_huis_Amersfoort_2.JPG/1280px-Mondriaan_huis_Amersfoort_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Mondriaan_huis_Amersfoort_2.JPG/500px-Mondriaan_huis_Amersfoort_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mondrian's birthplace in Amersfoort, Netherlands, now The Mondriaan House, a museum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMondriaan_huis_Amersfoort_2.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/2013-05-18_villa_mondrian.JPG/500px-2013-05-18_villa_mondrian.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/2013-05-18_villa_mondrian.JPG/1280px-2013-05-18_villa_mondrian.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/2013-05-18_villa_mondrian.JPG/500px-2013-05-18_villa_mondrian.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Piet Mondrian lived in this house, now the Villa Mondriaan, in Winterswijk, from 1880 to 1892.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2013-05-18_villa_mondrian.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/20_sep_13_klee_around_the_core.jpg/500px-20_sep_13_klee_around_the_core.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail